-- What happened to an issue, and when, and who did it.
--
-- Append-only in all but one narrow, owner-only respect. Nothing here is ever
-- deleted except by the cascade when the issue itself goes, and *what happened*
-- — `kind`, `value_text`, the child values — is never rewritten by anyone. That
-- is the whole point of the table: a history whose events you can re-word is not
-- a history.
--
-- The one exception is `reattribute` below. The instance owner, and only them
-- (LNL-191 — it was any system administrator), may correct *who and when* a row
-- records — its author, its date and its agent label — never its content. It
-- exists for the reason update_issue and update_comment carry owner-only
-- backfill parameters: history imported from
-- another tracker lands under a placeholder author, and the day that person gets
-- a real account the CREATED and STATUS_CHANGED events they left behind have to
-- be reattachable to it, or the audit trail names a stranger forever. See
-- McpTools.updateHistoryEvent and AccessControl.canAttributeWrites. Every other
-- caller writes only through `insert`; there is still no general `update`, and
-- no `delete`, to reach for by accident.
--
-- ── Why `kind` and not a sentence ──────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- The obvious cheap version of this table is one TEXT column holding "Robert
-- changed the title to Fix the login bug". It renders itself, needs no enum and
-- no join. It is also unreadable by anything that is not a human eye: you
-- cannot filter it, cannot count it, cannot re-word it after the fact, and
-- cannot translate it. Worse, the sentence is written by the build that
-- happened to be deployed that day, so a phrasing change makes the history
-- speak in two voices forever.
--
-- So the row stores the *fact* and the client writes the sentence. `kind` is
-- the fact's shape, and `value_text` / `value_user_id` / issue_event_values
-- hold whatever that shape carries.
--
-- ── Why kind is TEXT and not an integer ────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Because the constant name is already the wire format. kotlinx.serialization
-- encodes an enum by its name, so `IssueEventKind.TITLE_CHANGED` travels to the
-- browser as "TITLE_CHANGED" whatever this column does; storing 3 here would
-- add a second spelling, a mapping table to keep in step with it, and a class
-- of bug where the two disagree and every historical row shifts by one. See
-- AuthProvider in SessionState.kt, which settled the same question the same
-- way. The cost is a few bytes a row on a table nobody joins against.
--
-- An unrecognised kind is possible — a row written by a newer build and read by
-- an older one — and is handled by dropping it on the read, not by refusing the
-- issue. See IssueEventStore.forIssue.

CREATE TABLE issue_events (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,

    -- No project_id, for Comments.sq's reason: an event reaches its project
    -- through its issue, and a second copy here would be a second source of
    -- truth for a fact that already has one.
    issue_id INTEGER NOT NULL,

    -- The enum name. See the preamble.
    kind TEXT NOT NULL,

    -- What to render, when the kind carries a single value: the new title for
    -- TITLE_CHANGED, the status's name for STATUS_CHANGED, the assignee's name
    -- for ASSIGNEE_CHANGED. NULL for the kinds that carry nothing — CREATED and
    -- DESCRIPTION_CHANGED — and, meaningfully, for an ASSIGNEE_CHANGED that
    -- unassigned the issue.
    --
    -- ── Why a snapshot, and not a foreign key to the status or label ────────
    --
    -- Because a project's vocabulary is mutable in both directions: the settings
    -- dialog renames statuses and labels, and deletes them. A foreign key from a
    -- history row would force a choice between two wrong answers. SQLite's
    -- default RESTRICT would make any label that ever appeared in history
    -- permanently undeletable — the board would accumulate words nobody can
    -- remove, for no reason a user could ever see. ON DELETE CASCADE would
    -- silently rewrite the past: an event that read "labels: Bug, Feature"
    -- becomes "labels: Feature" the day somebody tidies the board, and no
    -- warning is printed because from the database's side nothing went wrong.
    --
    -- The snapshot is also the truer claim. The issue was moved into the column
    -- *then called* "In progress". If that column is renamed to "Doing"
    -- tomorrow, the sentence "moved to Doing" describes an event that did not
    -- happen. Renaming a column is not a retroactive edit of everyone's Tuesday.
    --
    -- The cost, stated plainly: a rename does not propagate, so history and
    -- board can name the same column differently. That is the intended
    -- behaviour, not the shortfall.
    value_text TEXT,

    -- The *person* an ASSIGNEE_CHANGED event points at, and nothing else.
    --
    -- The opposite call to value_text above, on purpose. A vocabulary word is a
    -- project's choice of language and belongs to the moment; a person is a
    -- person. Renaming yourself does not change who was handed the work, so an
    -- account stays a live reference and re-resolves on read — matching how
    -- CommentView.authorName is already resolved server-side from users rather
    -- than stored.
    --
    -- SET NULL rather than CASCADE, for created_by's reason one column down:
    -- deleting an account must not delete the history it appears in.
    --
    -- ── The pair of columns is what makes "unassigned" legible ──────────────
    --
    -- ASSIGNEE_CHANGED sets both: this id, and the name in value_text. That
    -- looks redundant and is not, because SET NULL will eventually empty this
    -- column and the two states it would collapse into are genuinely different
    -- events:
    --
    --   value_text NULL  → the issue was unassigned. Nobody was named.
    --   value_text set, value_user_id NULL → somebody was named, and that
    --                      account has since been deleted.
    --
    -- Without the snapshot both read as "assigned to nobody" and the history
    -- lies about one of them. So value_text is the discriminator, and
    -- value_user_id is the upgrade: when the account still exists, the reader
    -- prefers its current name over the snapshot.
    value_user_id INTEGER,

    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,

    -- Who did it. These three columns and the CHECK below are copied from
    -- issues, deliberately and exactly — same names, same nullability, same
    -- exclusivity rule. The long version of why each one is shaped this way
    -- lives in Issues.sq and is not repeated here.
    --
    -- Copied rather than re-derived because the degradation has to match. A
    -- deleted account leaves a null author on its issues and on its events, and
    -- both render as the same "A deleted account" through the same resolver. Two
    -- similar-but-different author models would eventually disagree about a
    -- deleted user on the very screen that shows both at once.
    created_by INTEGER,
    created_by_external TEXT,

    -- The agent that made the change on the author's behalf. Not a third kind
    -- of author; see issues.agent_name.
    --
    -- Worth one line of its own here: this column is why an event is written at
    -- all rather than inferred from the issue's own columns. `issues.agent_name`
    -- holds whoever last touched the issue through MCP, so it cannot answer
    -- "was *this particular* change made by an agent". The event can.
    agent_name TEXT,

    -- Which relation kind a RELATION_ADDED or RELATION_REMOVED event was about, as
    -- the kind's NAME stood at the time — "Blocked by", never an id and never an
    -- enum constant (LNL-215). NULL for every other kind, which is every event this
    -- table held before relations existed.
    --
    -- ── A snapshot, for value_text's reasons exactly ────────────────────────
    --
    -- Relation kinds are user-editable vocabulary now (see IssueRelationKinds.sq), so
    -- a reference here would face the same two wrong answers value_text describes at
    -- length: RESTRICT would make any kind that ever appeared in a history
    -- permanently undeletable, and CASCADE would silently rewrite the past. The
    -- snapshot is also the truer claim — the link was made under the label it was
    -- then called, and renaming that label is not a retroactive edit of anyone's
    -- Tuesday.
    --
    -- ── Which side's label ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    --
    -- The label for THAT ISSUE'S SIDE. One relation writes two events, one on each
    -- issue: the from-side issue's event carries the kind's `name` ("Blocked by") and
    -- the to-side issue's carries its `inverse_name` ("Blocks") — or `name` again
    -- when the kind is symmetric. So each history reads as a sentence about the issue
    -- it belongs to, which is the only reading that makes "linked this as Blocks
    -- LNL-9" appear on the right issue.
    --
    -- ── Why not issue_event_values ──────────────────────────────────────────
    --
    -- Because that child table is documented as carrying THE WHOLE SET a kind refers
    -- to — absolute values precisely so a gap in the history does not corrupt
    -- everything after it. Using it as a positional two-element tuple (kind, target)
    -- would contradict its own stated contract, and the first reader to assume the
    -- documented meaning would read a relation kind as a label.
    value_relation_kind TEXT,

    FOREIGN KEY (issue_id) REFERENCES issues(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (created_by) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
    FOREIGN KEY (value_user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,

    -- One author or the other, never both. See issues.
    CHECK (created_by IS NULL OR created_by_external IS NULL)
);

-- The issue detail view reads every event for one issue and nothing else ever
-- reads this table, so this is the only access path there is. Without it the
-- history section is a full scan of every event in the instance, which is fine
-- for a week and quietly is not later — this table grows monotonically and
-- faster than `issues` does, because one issue produces many events.
CREATE INDEX issue_events_issue_idx ON issue_events (issue_id, id);

-- The set-valued half of an event: the labels, or the components, as they stood
-- immediately after the change.
--
-- ── Why a child table rather than a delimited string ───────────────────────
--
-- Because a label may contain a comma. Every delimiter is a character somebody
-- is allowed to type, so a joined string needs an escaping scheme, and an
-- escaping scheme in a column is a parser that has to agree with itself across
-- every version of the app that ever wrote a row. JSON in a TEXT column is the
-- same bargain with extra steps.
--
-- ── The whole set, not a diff ──────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- A row here is "the labels were, from this moment, exactly these" — not "Bug
-- was added". Storing the delta would make every event depend on every earlier
-- event being present and correct to be read at all, so one gap in the history
-- corrupts everything after it, and the backfill in 5.sqm opens exactly such a
-- gap by design: it writes CREATED for issues whose intervening edits were never
-- recorded. Absolute values degrade gracefully across that gap. Deltas do not.
--
-- The reader may still show a delta by comparing against the previous event of
-- the same kind — that is a rendering choice, and it can be changed later
-- precisely because the stored form did not commit to it.
CREATE TABLE issue_event_values (
    event_id INTEGER NOT NULL,

    -- Ordering, so the set comes back the way it was written rather than in
    -- whatever order SQLite finds convenient. A history that reshuffles its own
    -- labels between two page loads looks broken even though nothing changed.
    position INTEGER NOT NULL,

    -- A name snapshot, for value_text's reasons exactly.
    value TEXT NOT NULL,

    PRIMARY KEY (event_id, position),
    FOREIGN KEY (event_id) REFERENCES issue_events(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

-- Append one event. The values, if any, follow through insertValue.
--
-- RETURNING id because the caller needs it to write those values, and a
-- last_insert_rowid() round-trip would be a second statement that a concurrent
-- writer could get between.
insert:
INSERT INTO issue_events (issue_id, kind, value_text, value_user_id, created_at, created_by, created_by_external, agent_name, value_relation_kind)
VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)
RETURNING id;

insertValue:
INSERT INTO issue_event_values (event_id, position, value) VALUES (?, ?, ?);

-- Oldest first: this is read as a narrative, and a narrative runs forwards.
--
-- Ordered by id, not created_at. They almost always agree, and when they do not
-- it is because two events landed in the same millisecond — at which point
-- created_at is a tie SQLite breaks arbitrarily and differently between runs,
-- so the history would reorder itself under a reload. `id` is monotonic by
-- construction and is the order the events actually happened in. Same-
-- millisecond pairs are not hypothetical: publishing a draft writes CREATED and
-- the editor's field events together.
forIssue:
SELECT id, issue_id, kind, value_text, value_user_id, created_at, created_by, created_by_external, agent_name, value_relation_kind
FROM issue_events WHERE issue_id = ? ORDER BY id;

-- One event by id, for the admin reattribution path — it needs the row's issue
-- (to gate the caller against that project) and the event's current author, date
-- and agent label (to default whatever the call leaves unspoken). The child
-- values are not fetched: reattribution never touches them, so this deliberately
-- does not do forIssue's second query. See McpTools.updateHistoryEvent.
findById:
SELECT id, issue_id, kind, value_text, value_user_id, created_at, created_by, created_by_external, agent_name, value_relation_kind
FROM issue_events WHERE id = ?;

-- Correct who and when an event records — never what it records. `kind`,
-- `value_text`, `value_user_id` and the child values in issue_event_values are
-- all untouched, so the change stays exactly what it always was and only its
-- attribution moves. The narrative order is unaffected: forIssue sorts by `id`,
-- not created_at, so a re-dated event stays where it happened rather than
-- reshuffling the history. Admin-only, and the only UPDATE this table has — see
-- the preamble and McpTools.updateHistoryEvent.
reattribute:
UPDATE issue_events
SET created_at = ?, created_by = ?, created_by_external = ?, agent_name = ?
WHERE id = ?;

-- The values for one issue's events, in one query rather than one per event —
-- an issue with fifty events would otherwise be fifty round-trips to render a
-- sidebar. Joined back to issue_events because issue_event_values has no
-- issue_id of its own, for the reason issue_id gives above.
--
-- Ordered so the caller can group by event_id in a single pass.
valuesForIssue:
SELECT v.event_id, v.value
FROM issue_event_values v
JOIN issue_events e ON e.id = v.event_id
WHERE e.issue_id = ?
ORDER BY v.event_id, v.position;

-- One issue's whole history, deleted — the events and, a step behind them, their
-- values.
--
-- The append-only rule's other exception, and the one that is not an admin
-- correction: the issue these events describe is going, so there is nothing left
-- for them to be the history of.
--
-- Redundant here and deliberately so, as Attachments.sq's `deleteForIssue` is:
-- `issue_events.issue_id` is ON DELETE CASCADE and `issue_event_values.event_id`
-- cascades a second step from it, so both tables would empty a moment later
-- anyway. It exists because the Firestore backend has no cascade and
-- `IssueRepository.delete` is written once for both. See LNL-177.
--
-- The values go first: deleting the parents first would leave this statement
-- nothing to join against, and while the FK cascade would have taken them, the
-- point of these statements is to not depend on that.
deleteValuesForIssue:
DELETE FROM issue_event_values
WHERE event_id IN (SELECT id FROM issue_events WHERE issue_id = ?);

deleteForIssue:
DELETE FROM issue_events WHERE issue_id = ?;
